PHASE 1: Problem Discovery
[ ] Identify the problem
[ ] Identify the affected users and stakeholders
[ ] Understand the current process
[ ] Identify pain points and root causes
[ ] Determine whether software is the appropriate solution
[ ] Research existing solutions and alternatives
[ ] Gather initial observations and evidence

PHASE 2: Feasibility Study
[ ] Assess technical feasibility
[ ] Assess available time, budget, skills, and resources
[ ] Identify legal, privacy, accessibility, and security considerations
[ ] Identify major risks and dependencies
[ ] Evaluate possible solutions
[ ] Decide whether to proceed, revise, or stop

PHASE 3: Project Definition
[ ] Choose a project title
[ ] Write the problem statement
[ ] Define the project purpose
[ ] Define measurable objectives
[ ] Identify target users and stakeholders
[ ] Define the project scope
[ ] Define what is out of scope
[ ] Document assumptions and limitations
[ ] Define success criteria
[ ] Identify initial deliverables

PHASE 4: Requirements Gathering
[ ] Gather user and stakeholder needs
[ ] List functional requirements
[ ] List non-functional requirements
[ ] Define business rules
[ ] Define inputs, processes, and expected outputs
[ ] Identify data requirements
[ ] Define user roles and permissions
[ ] Identify error and exception scenarios
[ ] Identify accessibility and usability requirements
[ ] Prioritize requirements
[ ] Write user stories or use cases where useful
[ ] Define acceptance criteria

PHASE 5: Requirements Analysis and Approval
[ ] Clarify ambiguous requirements
[ ] Resolve conflicting requirements
[ ] Identify missing requirements
[ ] Map user workflows
[ ] Identify edge cases
[ ] Check that requirements are testable
[ ] Assess requirement feasibility
[ ] Document requirements and constraints
[ ] Review requirements with stakeholders or instructor
[ ] Obtain approval or agreement where required
[ ] Establish a requirements baseline
[ ] Define how future changes will be evaluated and approved

PHASE 6: Project Planning
[ ] Choose a development approach, such as Waterfall or Agile
[ ] Break the project into milestones and tasks
[ ] Estimate time and effort
[ ] Set deadlines and priorities
[ ] Identify task dependencies
[ ] Assign responsibilities if working in a team
[ ] Choose task-tracking and communication tools
[ ] Create a risk register with mitigation plans
[ ] Plan progress reviews
[ ] Define the project's Definition of Done

PHASE 7: Technical Decisions
[ ] Choose the programming language
[ ] Choose frameworks and libraries if needed
[ ] Choose the runtime and supported platforms
[ ] Choose the data storage method
[ ] Choose the IDE and development tools
[ ] Evaluate dependencies and third-party services
[ ] Check compatibility requirements
[ ] Review licenses and maintenance risks
[ ] Document important technical decisions

PHASE 8: Solution Analysis
[ ] Identify inputs, processes, and outputs using IPO where useful
[ ] Break the problem into smaller subproblems
[ ] Identify variables and data types
[ ] Determine required data structures
[ ] Define the main algorithm
[ ] Identify conditions, loops, and calculations
[ ] Identify validation rules
[ ] Identify possible failures and error handling
[ ] Determine function, module, or class responsibilities
[ ] Prepare initial test scenarios

PHASE 9: System and Solution Design
[ ] Design the solution architecture appropriate to project size
[ ] Write pseudocode where useful or required
[ ] Create a flowchart where useful or required
[ ] Design functions, modules, and class interfaces
[ ] Design data models and relationships
[ ] Design the database schema if applicable
[ ] Design APIs and data contracts if applicable
[ ] Create user flows and wireframes if there is a UI
[ ] Create a prototype if useful
[ ] Design navigation and responsive layouts if applicable
[ ] Consider accessibility
[ ] Design authentication and authorization if applicable
[ ] Plan logging and error reporting
[ ] Plan backup and recovery for important data
[ ] Document technical and design decisions

PHASE 10: Design Review and Validation
[ ] Check the design against the requirements
[ ] Check the algorithm using sample inputs
[ ] Verify expected outputs manually or with trace tables
[ ] Review edge cases
[ ] Review maintainability and complexity
[ ] Assess security and privacy risks
[ ] Review usability and accessibility
[ ] Revise the design where needed
[ ] Obtain design approval if required

PHASE 11: Development Environment and Repository Setup
[ ] Create the project directory
[ ] Initialize a Git repository or required version control
[ ] Choose a clear folder and file structure
[ ] Create the initial README
[ ] Install the required language runtime and tools
[ ] Create an isolated environment if appropriate
[ ] Install and manage dependencies
[ ] Create a dependency manifest or lockfile where appropriate
[ ] Configure formatting and linting tools where useful
[ ] Configure static analysis or type checking where appropriate
[ ] Configure environment variables safely
[ ] Create an example configuration file without secrets
[ ] Create a .gitignore file
[ ] Configure backups or remote repository access where appropriate
[ ] Verify that the initial project runs

PHASE 12: Implementation Planning
[ ] Break the design into small implementation tasks
[ ] Choose the order of implementation
[ ] Identify task dependencies
[ ] Define function and module interfaces
[ ] Prepare sample data and test fixtures
[ ] Prepare test cases and expected results
[ ] Decide what needs unit, integration, and system testing
[ ] Define code conventions and review expectations

PHASE 13: Coding and Implementation
[ ] Implement one small feature or module at a time
[ ] Follow the approved requirements and design
[ ] Use meaningful variable, function, and class names
[ ] Follow appropriate coding conventions
[ ] Use suitable data structures and algorithms
[ ] Write reusable functions where useful
[ ] Apply appropriate object-oriented or procedural design
[ ] Validate user input
[ ] Implement error handling
[ ] Avoid unnecessary duplication and complexity
[ ] Add comments for non-obvious logic where useful
[ ] Keep secrets out of source code
[ ] Use safe handling of files, databases, and external input
[ ] Commit changes using version control
[ ] Run the program frequently
[ ] Update documentation when behavior changes

PHASE 14: Continuous Testing and Debugging
[ ] Run the program after meaningful changes
[ ] Test individual functions and modules
[ ] Test normal inputs
[ ] Test invalid and missing inputs
[ ] Test boundary values and edge cases
[ ] Compare actual outputs against expected results
[ ] Test error handling
[ ] Test file and database operations if applicable
[ ] Test module integration
[ ] Test API behavior if applicable
[ ] Test authentication and authorization if applicable
[ ] Reproduce reported bugs
[ ] Identify root causes
[ ] Fix defects without introducing regressions
[ ] Rerun relevant tests after each fix
[ ] Record test results and unresolved bugs

PHASE 15: Code Review and Quality Assurance
[ ] Review code correctness and readability
[ ] Check naming, structure, and maintainability
[ ] Review duplicated or unnecessarily complex logic
[ ] Run formatting and linting checks where available
[ ] Run static analysis or type checks where appropriate
[ ] Review dependencies and known vulnerabilities
[ ] Check error handling and sensitive-data exposure
[ ] Perform peer review where appropriate
[ ] Perform regression testing
[ ] Refactor when it improves maintainability
[ ] Confirm that acceptance criteria are met
[ ] Record outstanding quality issues

PHASE 16: Security, Privacy, and Compliance Verification
[ ] Review the system's threat model where appropriate
[ ] Check access controls and permissions
[ ] Verify secure handling of credentials and secrets
[ ] Check input validation and output encoding
[ ] Review database queries and file access for unsafe operations
[ ] Check data minimization and retention practices
[ ] Verify encryption requirements where applicable
[ ] Review third-party components and licenses
[ ] Run suitable security checks for the project's risk level
[ ] Confirm applicable privacy and legal obligations
[ ] Fix or formally assess security findings before release

PHASE 17: Documentation and User Instructions
[ ] Complete the README
[ ] Document prerequisites and installation
[ ] Document how to configure and run the program
[ ] Document features and usage instructions
[ ] Document configuration variables without exposing secrets
[ ] Document dependencies and setup requirements
[ ] Document APIs where applicable
[ ] Document database setup and migrations where applicable
[ ] Document known limitations
[ ] Prepare pseudocode and flowcharts if required
[ ] Prepare test cases and test results
[ ] Prepare user manuals or technical documentation where required
[ ] Prepare project report and presentation if required
[ ] Prepare screenshots or demonstration materials if required
[ ] Update documentation to match the actual implementation

PHASE 18: Build and Packaging
[ ] Choose the appropriate build or packaging method
[ ] Generate the application or submission package
[ ] Include required source files and supporting assets
[ ] Include dependency and configuration templates
[ ] Include required database migrations or sample data
[ ] Exclude credentials, temporary files, caches, and unnecessary local environments
[ ] Set a version number where appropriate
[ ] Prepare release notes
[ ] Verify the package contents
[ ] Test installation or execution in a clean environment where feasible
[ ] Verify that another person can follow the setup instructions

PHASE 19: Release Readiness and Acceptance
[ ] Confirm all required features are complete
[ ] Verify acceptance criteria
[ ] Confirm relevant tests pass
[ ] Review open bugs and known limitations
[ ] Confirm security and privacy checks are complete for the project's risk level
[ ] Verify backups and recovery procedures where necessary
[ ] Confirm deployment configuration
[ ] Prepare rollback or recovery steps where appropriate
[ ] Obtain required stakeholder or instructor approval
[ ] Confirm the exact version to be delivered

PHASE 20: Deployment or Final Submission
[ ] Choose the target environment
[ ] Configure production settings securely if deploying
[ ] Provision hosting, database, and infrastructure if applicable
[ ] Deploy the approved version or prepare the school submission
[ ] Run smoke tests in the target environment
[ ] Verify application access and core features
[ ] Check data handling and configuration
[ ] Verify the correct files were submitted
[ ] Provide installation and usage instructions
[ ] Provide a demonstration or handover if required
[ ] Record the version and delivery date

PHASE 21: Operations and Monitoring
[ ] Monitor application errors and availability if deployed
[ ] Review logs and alerts
[ ] Track performance and resource usage where relevant
[ ] Collect user feedback
[ ] Protect logs and avoid recording unnecessary sensitive data
[ ] Verify backup completion where applicable
[ ] Periodically test data restoration where appropriate
[ ] Maintain operational and incident-response instructions
[ ] Respond to incidents and document their resolution

PHASE 22: Maintenance and Improvement
[ ] Fix reported bugs
[ ] Patch security vulnerabilities
[ ] Update dependencies responsibly
[ ] Maintain compatibility with supported platforms
[ ] Improve performance when justified
[ ] Improve usability based on feedback
[ ] Add approved features
[ ] Run regression tests after changes
[ ] Update documentation and release notes
[ ] Release and verify updates
[ ] Reassess risks after significant changes

PHASE 23: Project Review and Closure
[ ] Compare final results with the original objectives
[ ] Confirm final deliverables are complete
[ ] Obtain final acceptance where required
[ ] Record lessons learned
[ ] Review schedule, quality, and project risks
[ ] Document remaining issues and technical debt
[ ] Archive code, documents, and important decisions
[ ] Transfer ownership and access where applicable
[ ] Remove obsolete credentials and access
[ ] Plan continued support or formal closure

PHASE 24: Retirement and Decommissioning
[ ] Decide when the software should be retired
[ ] Notify affected users and stakeholders
[ ] Plan migration or export of needed data
[ ] Back up or retain records according to applicable requirements
[ ] Revoke obsolete accounts, tokens, and credentials
[ ] Remove obsolete services and infrastructure
[ ] Delete data securely when authorized and appropriate
[ ] Update documentation and asset records
[ ] Confirm decommissioning is complete
